    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let the two numbers be $x$ and $y$, where $x > y$. We are given that:\n\n1. $x + y = 6$\n2. $x^2 - y^2 = 12$\n\nTo solve for $x$ and $y$, we can use the difference of squares formula: $x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)$. Substituting the values from the given equations, we have:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nSince $x + y = 6$ from the first equation, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 6, we get:\n\n\\[\nx - y = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we have a system of linear equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx + y = 6 \\\\\nx - y = 2\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAdding these two equations together:\n\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2x = 8\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nx = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstituting $x = 4$ into the first equation:\n\n\\[\n4 + y = 6\n\\]\n\nSolving for $y$:\n\n\\[\ny = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are 4 and 2. The positive difference between these two numbers is:\n\n\\[\n4 - 2 = \\boxed{2}\n\\]",
