      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We are given two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6.\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. The difference of their squares is 12.\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), we start with the second equation and use the difference of squares formula, which states:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n   \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   12 = (x + y)(x - y) = 6(x - y)\n   \\]\n\nDividing both sides by 6:\n   \\[\n   x - y = 2\n   \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 6 \\)\n2. \\( x - y = 2 \\)\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations together:\n   \\[\n   (x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2x = 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 4\n   \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = 4 \\) back into the equation \\( x + y = 6 \\):\n   \\[\n   4 + y = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 2\n   \\]\n\nThus, the two numbers are \\( x = 4 \\) and \\( y = 2 \\). The positive difference between these two numbers is:\n   \\[\n   x - y = 4 - 2 = 2\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
